# Question 1 -- Delete a Pod and Observe Self-Healing

## Objective

Deploy Nginx with 3 replicas, manually delete one Pod, observe a
replacement, and identify the controller responsible.

## Commands

Apply:

``` bash
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
```

Check Deployment:

``` bash
kubectl get deployment
```

Check Pods:

``` bash
kubectl get pods
```

Check ReplicaSet:

``` bash
kubectl get rs
```

Delete one Pod:

``` bash
kubectl delete pod <pod-name>
```

Watch Pods:

``` bash
kubectl get pods -w
```

## Expected behavior

Before deletion:

``` text
3 Pods Running
```

After deleting one:

``` text
2 Pods
```

The ReplicaSet detects that the desired count is 3 and creates a
replacement:

``` text
2 Pods
   |
ReplicaSet detects difference
   |
New Pod created
   |
3 Pods Running
```

## Key learning

The **ReplicaSet controller** performs the replacement.

Deployment manages the ReplicaSet.

