# Question 5 -- Deploy a Broken Image and Observe a Stalled Rollout

## Objective

Deploy an invalid image and understand what a stalled rollout looks
like.

Change the image to an intentionally invalid image:

``` yaml
image: nginx:doesnotexist
```

Apply:

``` bash
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
```

Watch:

``` bash
kubectl get pods -w
```

Check rollout:

``` bash
kubectl rollout status deployment nginx-deployment
```

Use a timeout for the lab:

``` bash
kubectl rollout status deployment nginx-deployment --timeout=60s
```

The new Pod may show:

``` text
ErrImagePull
```

or:

``` text
ImagePullBackOff
```

Investigate:

``` bash
kubectl describe pod <new-pod-name>
kubectl get rs
kubectl describe deployment nginx-deployment
```

## Healthy rollout

``` text
New Pods become Ready
        |
        v
Rollout completes
```

## Stalled rollout

``` text
New Pods fail
     |
     v
Pods do not become Ready
     |
     v
Deployment waits
     |
     v
Rollout does not complete
```

## Important

`ImagePullBackOff` is an image-pull problem.

`CrashLoopBackOff` is different: the container starts but repeatedly
crashes.

