# Question 6 + 7 -- Revision History and Specific Rollback

## Objective

Create several revisions, inspect revision history, identify a specific
revision, roll back to it, and verify the result.

This combines rollout history and rollback into one practical exercise.

## Step 1 -- Create revisions

Example:

``` bash
kubectl set image deployment nginx-deployment nginx=nginx:1.26
kubectl rollout status deployment nginx-deployment
```

Then:

``` bash
kubectl set image deployment nginx-deployment nginx=nginx:1.27
kubectl rollout status deployment nginx-deployment
```

Then:

``` bash
kubectl set image deployment nginx-deployment nginx=nginx:1.28
kubectl rollout status deployment nginx-deployment
```

## Step 2 -- View history

``` bash
kubectl rollout history deployment nginx-deployment
```

Example:

``` text
REVISION
11
12
13
```

Your actual revision numbers may be different.

## Step 3 -- Inspect a specific revision

Example:

``` bash
kubectl rollout history deployment nginx-deployment --revision=11
```

This can show the Pod template and image associated with that revision.

## Step 4 -- Roll back

Example:

``` bash
kubectl rollout undo deployment nginx-deployment --to-revision=11
```

Check:

``` bash
kubectl rollout status deployment nginx-deployment
```

Verify image:

``` bash
kubectl get deployment nginx-deployment \
  -o=jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}'
```

Verify Pods:

``` bash
kubectl get pods
```

## Key learning

``` text
Update
  |
New revision
  |
Another update
  |
Another revision
  |
rollout history
  |
Select revision
  |
rollout undo --to-revision=N
  |
Rollback
```

Always use the revision number actually returned by your cluster.

