# Question 8 -- RollingUpdate vs Recreate

## Objective

Compare RollingUpdate and Recreate behavior.

## RollingUpdate example

``` yaml
strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate
  rollingUpdate:
    maxSurge: 1
    maxUnavailable: 1
```

Conceptually:

``` text
Old Old Old Old
       |
Old Old Old New
       |
Old Old New New
       |
Old New New New
       |
New New New New
```

Old and new Pods can temporarily coexist.

## Recreate example

``` yaml
strategy:
  type: Recreate
```

Conceptually:

``` text
Old Old Old
     |
   No Pods
     |
New New New
```

All old Pods are terminated before new Pods are created.

## Practical comparison

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  RollingUpdate                       Recreate
  ----------------------------------- -----------------------------------
  Gradually replaces Pods             Terminates all old Pods first

  Old/new versions can coexist        Old/new versions do not coexist

  Designed to maintain availability   Causes a downtime window

  Uses maxSurge/maxUnavailable        These RollingUpdate settings do not
                                      apply

  Common for web applications         Useful when versions cannot coexist
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

## Measuring downtime

Watch:

``` bash
kubectl get pods -w
```

Record when the old Pods disappear and when the new Pods become Ready.

For a realistic application test, use a Service and observe its
endpoints:

``` bash
kubectl get endpoints <service-name> -w
```

Do not use example downtime numbers as actual measurements. Record the
timing from your own cluster.

